#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Merge each reviewer-approved pull request in turn, oldest first.

The Merger owns the default branch (`main` below): nobody else merges.
A pull request is the Merger's once a reviewer agent's App has approved
it at a head the approval
still covers, nobody has spoken since, and nothing it names in a
`Blocked-by:` line is still open. The Merger takes those in order:

* with `merge.test_ahead`, those already predicted, in the order
  predicted (below);
* with `labels.urgent`, the pull requests carrying that label come
  next, oldest-opened first (a person's call: it goes before
  blockers), and with them those that fix a red nightly
  (designs/0028): while forge-nightly's issue saying so is open, each
  open pull request that closes it, read from forge-nightly's state
  (agent_forge/nightly.py), and those that fix a red default branch
  (designs/0011, amendment): while forge-pr-check's issue saying so is
  open, each open pull request that closes it, read from its
  state.json (`main_red.fixes`). These go ahead of the predicted ones
  too, behind only the one in flight and a green batch still merging;
* then the pull requests another open one is blocked by, held or not;
* then the rest, oldest-opened first;
* with `merge.yield_after_hand_back`, each that was handed back lately
  then lets others in its group go first (below).

For the first candidate, it decides one action:

* `UPDATE`: behind `main`, and merging `main` into it is clean;
* `UNION`: behind `main`, and merging `main` into it conflicts only
  where both sides added lines, in files `merge.resolve.union` names
  (designs/0046, below);
* `WAIT`: the build check (`checks.context`) has not reported on the
  head, or a run of the CI workflow on it has not finished;
* `MERGE`: every gate check passed;
* `HAND-BACK`: a check failed. The record says to whom, the author or
  the approving Reviewer, and why;
* `SKIP`: the question can't be answered this tick (a fetch failed, or
  the head moved while reading). The next tick asks again.

**Without `--dry-run` it acts**, as the Merger's own App. A tick makes
at most one update or merge, and a run whose tick merged ticks again
at once (at most TICKS_PER_RUN ticks), so the next pull request is
updated without waiting for the next event or cron tick. Before it, it hands back each pull
request ahead in the queue that it can't take, unless that hand-back
was already said:

* `UPDATE` asks GitHub to merge `main` into the head (`update-branch`,
  pinned to the head it judged), then says on the pull request
  (UPDATE_NOTE) that the approval still covers it, so nobody asks for
  a review again;
* `MERGE` squash-merges, pinned the same way, with GitHub's default
  message. A later live tick, `CLOSE_AFTER` on, closes each issue the
  pull request's `closingIssuesReferences` names that GitHub left
  open, with a comment naming the merge, and prints `CLOSED-ISSUE`
  (`closing.json` in the state directory; one reopened since, or in
  another repository, is left alone). GitHub does this itself, but
  not always;
* `HAND-BACK` comments once per approval and head, whatever the
  reason (`merge.hand_back_once_per_head`, designs/0044; without it,
  once per approval, head and reason), signed `— Merger`. To the author it is a plain comment, and for a conflict
  it says what to do (CONFLICT_ADVICE) and lists the author's other
  open pull requests with where each stands; to the approving
  Reviewer it starts `Not-a-review`, since it asks for a review rather
  than giving one.

**A head that conflicts with `main` goes back to its author, once, and
the tick moves on to the next pull request.** `git merge-tree` finds
the conflict before any write, so a conflicted head is never sent to
`update-branch` at all. If GitHub still refuses an update as a merge
conflict, the same hand-back follows in the same tick and the queue
moves on; that head is not sent to `update-branch` again until a new
approval or head. A conflict is never retried: retrying one every tick stalls
everything queued behind it. Any other write GitHub refuses is
recorded, not retried in the same tick: the next tick judges the pull
request afresh.

**With `merge.resolve_slots: N`, at most N are out for resolving at
once** (default 0, no limit; designs/0012, amendment of 2026-11-12).
One is out while it was handed back for a conflict with `main` (a
restack's conflict too) at the head and under the approval it has
now, isn't a draft, and still conflicts; its author's push, a new or
lost approval, closing, merging, or `main` moving so that it merges
cleanly frees the slot, and its re-review after the fix holds none.
Every other conflict the tick finds, at the front, behind it with
`early_conflicts`, or refused by GitHub, waits for the tick's end. Then
the free slots go, in turn, to those labelled urgent, the other urgent
ones, the one with right of way, then to those that would board
soonest (their place in the queue, by `test_ahead.batch` when there
are batches, and within that, a stack's base with more approved
layers above it first), then the oldest; each gets its hand-back as
before. The rest are held, untold, with nothing in `handed-back.json`:
`held` says `conflicts with main; waits for a resolve slot (2 out:
#a, #b)`, they leave the record's `queue` and every prediction, and
one `RESOLVE-WAIT #c #d: ...` line is printed. The record says
`resolving` (those out) and `resolve_waiting`. A resolution done while
others merge is often undone by the next merge; a slot keeps the few
being resolved near the front, where they board soon after. A stack's
layers are held as stacked while their base is out, as before, and a
pull request held behind another (`hold_conflicting`, right of way)
isn't in the queue the slots are given from.

**A head GitHub has lost is nudged once.** GitHub sometimes moves a
pull request's branch (its own update's merge commit lands on it)
without moving the pull request's head, and then refuses every update
or merge pinned to either, saying the expected head sha didn't match.
On that refusal the Merger reads the pull request's head and its
branch's ref. When they differ, and it hasn't done so for this pull
request and head before (`resynced.json` in the state directory), it
sets the pull request's base to the base it has, a write that changes
nothing but makes GitHub read the head again, reads the pull request
once more and prints `RESYNC #N: GitHub had head <sha>, branch is at
<sha>; nudged with a no-op base update, and GitHub now has <sha>`. A
pull request GitHub now holds another head for is a changed pull
request: the record says `RESYNC`, the tick moves on, and the next tick
judges the new head as it would any other (its approval, its check),
never the head GitHub lost.

**A CI run GitHub misreports is judged by its jobs.** GitHub sometimes
goes on reporting a run of `merge.ci_workflow` as `queued` or
`in_progress` long after every job in it has completed, while it
considers the run finished itself (it refuses to cancel it). For each
run on the head that says it is unfinished, the Merger reads its jobs
(every page): when all have completed, the run is finished, green when
each job concluded `success`, `skipped` or `neutral` and red (handed
back like any red run) otherwise, and the tick prints `CI-STALE #N:
GitHub says run <id> is in_progress, but all its jobs finished at
<time>; judged by its jobs (green)`. A run whose jobs say unfinished
too is waited on, as before. Once it has been unfinished for
`STALL_AFTER` since it started, the Merger re-runs it, once per pull
request and head (`ci-reruns.json` in the state directory), printing
`CI-RERUN #N: ...`; a dry run only says it would. If the head's CI is
still unfinished `STALL_AFTER` after the re-run, the tick prints
`CI-STUCK #N: ...`, hands the pull request back to its author (once per
approval and head; a push starts CI afresh) and the queue moves past
it, until the run does finish. Like a lost head, that hand-back is not
the pull request's own failure.

Each tick appends one JSON record to `merger.jsonl` in the state
directory (~/.local/state/agent-forge/<project>/merger, or
`--state-dir`) (`dry-run.jsonl` under `--dry-run`). `held` names each pull
request Reviewer approved that is not eligible, with the reason;
`urgent` lists every open pull request the tick saw that carries
`labels.urgent`, and `elevated` those urgent only because a tool says
so (a red nightly's or default branch's fix); and
`handed-back.json` remembers what was already said.

**With `merge.yield_after_hand_back: N`, a pull request handed back
lately lets others go first** (default off). Otherwise one handed back
to its author for its own failure (a red check, a conflict, a failed
gate) goes
straight back to the front once it is fixed and approved again, and if
it fails again everything behind it waits another update and build,
every prediction behind it wasted. With it, a candidate handed back K
times in the last 24 hours (to its author, distinct heads and reasons
in this state directory's record, K at most `merge.yield_cap`, default
3) is moved
back N times K places after the order above, less one for each other
pull request that has merged while it was a candidate since its latest
such hand-back (so the yield is spent, not a standing penalty), within
its group: a pull
request another is blocked by is never moved behind one that isn't,
and none past the group's end. A hand-back to the approving reviewer
(a fresh review is wanted), holds, waits and stall alerts don't count. With `test_ahead`, the pull request a run is in flight for
keeps its place and never yields. The record's `yielded` says who
moved back how many places.

**With `merge.stacks`, a stack of pull requests merges as a unit**
(designs/0025; default off). A pull request based on another open one's
head branch is held as `stacked on #Q`, so it can be approved while it
waits. When a live tick merges Q, the open pull requests stacked on it
are recorded in `stacks.json` in the state directory, with Q's head,
and the run goes on. The next tick retargets the first such layer that
is eligible but for its base to `main` (`RETARGET`, printing `STACK #N:
retargeted to main after #Q merged; K above`), as its only write. Once
on `main`, a layer goes first in the queue, after only the one a
`test_ahead` run is in flight for (or, while that run's batch doesn't
hold the layer, after that batch), and is updated, checked and merged
like any other; a merge of a merged layer's head into it, made after its
approval, covers its head like a merge of `main` when it is clean. A
layer that isn't eligible ends the stack and is held as usual, unless
`merge.stacks.wait_hours` is set (designs/0033): then it stays in
`stacks.json`, held as `stacked on #Q, which merged; waiting for: <why>`
and judged again every tick, and is retargeted once it is eligible but
for its base, keeping its usual place in the queue (the layers above it
still go first once it merges). After `wait_hours` it leaves the stack,
and the Merger says so once on it (the record's `stack_waited`). With
`max_run`, once that many layers merged in a row the next keeps its
usual place. The record's `stack` lists the layers still to come. A
layer whose base merged without the Merger (by hand on GitHub) is found
too: a pull request based on a branch no open pull request has as its
head is looked up among the closed ones from that branch, and if one
merged into `main` its layers join the stack as after the Merger's own
merge (`merged-elsewhere.json` in the state directory remembers each
branch asked about: one with no merged pull request for an hour, the
rest, and those the Merger merged, for 30 days, so a layer that left
its stack doesn't come back).

**`--dry-run` writes nothing to GitHub.** It reads with the Merger's
App too, since a dry run is how to watch the Merger before it acts.

**Which approvals count.** Only an `APPROVED` review by the App of an
agent the configuration marks `"kind": "reviewer"` (`APPROVERS`), that
did not open the pull request. Any reviewer's counts, whichever area
the pull request is in: the areas decide whom to ask, not whose word
counts.

**What "covers the head" means.** Call the approval's commit A and the
head H. Either H is A, or every commit reachable from H but not from A
or from `main` is a merge with these properties:

* one of its parents is on `main`;
* its tree equals `git merge-tree --write-tree` of its two parents,
  or, with `merge.resolve.union`, the resolution `UNION` makes of
  them (below), recomputed from the two parents in their order.

GitHub carries an approval forward across any push, so this check is
what stops an unreviewed commit being merged under it. An author's own
clean merge of `main` passes; a hand-resolved conflict does not, and
needs a new approval, unless it is exactly the resolution the Merger
would have made (`forge-resolve` makes it).

**With `merge.resolve.union`, a conflict of additions is resolved, not
handed back** (designs/0046; default off). It is a list of path globs
(`agent_forge/union.py`). When merging `main` into the head conflicts,
the Merger takes `git merge-tree --write-tree` of the head and `main`'s
tip, with each conflicting hunk shown with its base (`zdiff3`). Only
if every conflict is in a file's lines (no modify/delete, rename,
binary or mode conflict), in a file a glob matches, and every
conflicting hunk's base is empty, so both sides only added lines, is it
resolved: each hunk becomes the head's lines, then `main`'s. Anything
else anywhere and it is handed back as before, the comment saying why
the Merger didn't resolve it. A resolved merge is committed as the
Merger (parents: the head, then `main`'s tip), pushed to the branch
with a lease on the head it judged, at most once per approval and head
(`unioned.json` in the state directory), and announced in a comment,
signed `— Merger`, listing each file and the lines it kept from each
side. Then it waits for the build check on the new head, as after
`UPDATE`. A push GitHub refuses is judged afresh next tick; any other
failure hands the pull request back for the conflict. With
`merge.early_conflicts`, a candidate behind the front whose conflict
this resolves is not handed back early.

**Where that re-review starts.** The hand-back to the approver lists
the paths whose content in H a reviewer has not seen together with
`main`'s, so the re-review can start there rather than at the whole pull request. With
M the `main` commit H contains (their merge base) and B the merge base
of A and M, comparing mode and blob, a path is listed when H has:

* content from neither A nor M (a hand-merged path, or a commit after
  the approval), listed first;
* A's content where M has other content it gave the path itself: a
  resolution that kept the pull request's side;
* M's content where A had changed the path to something else: a
  resolution that took `main`'s side.

A path is not listed when H has A's content and `main` left it alone
or agrees, or H has M's content for a path A didn't change. The list
only points; it never lets a head through.

**What "nobody has spoken" means.** No comment or review after the
approval, except one carrying `Not-a-review` in the same text, one by
the Merger's own App (`QUIET_LOGINS`), one by the pull request's
author, or an approval: an `APPROVED` review, whatever it says, such as
an owner's sign-off for their part. An author who wants it held makes
it a draft, and a reviewer who has second thoughts requests changes.
Anything else said after the approval makes the pull request its
reviewer's again, until a newer approval. The Merger says so on the
pull request, once per approval and remark (`HELD_FILE`), so the
approver knows the hold is theirs to lift.

**A standing request for changes holds it.** A reviewer's (the
owner's, or anyone's who can push to the repository) newest verdict, `APPROVED`, `CHANGES_REQUESTED` or
`DISMISSED`, that is `CHANGES_REQUESTED` stands until that reviewer
reviews again or it is dismissed, whatever another reviewer approved
since: GitHub refuses the merge while it stands, so taking the pull
request would only be refused every tick.

**A `Defer-until:` holds it too**: the newest such line anywhere on the
pull request, `Not-a-review` comments included. A time in the future,
or a value that is not a time, holds it.

**The gate, on a head that contains `main`'s tip:**

* the newest commit status named `checks.context` on exactly that SHA
  is `success`. The build check posts it as the App `checks.poster`
  names (default: the Merger's own), and only statuses that App
  created count, so nobody else can post a green one;
* every run of the Actions workflow `merge.ci_workflow` (default `ci`)
  on it succeeded. `""` means the project has no such workflow;
* with `checks.instances.<name>.gate`, that instance's context is
  `success` on the head, or before the merges of `main` into it, for
  each pull request the instance applies to; until then the pull
  request is held, not waited on, so the queue moves past it
  (`instance_wait`);
* every gate tool in `merge.gate` exits 0. Each is an argument list
  with `{pr}`, `{base}` (main's tip) and `{head}` filled in, and an
  argument that is exactly `{below}` replaced by the number of a
  stack's merged layer below it (designs/0025), or left out when it
  isn't one; the
  default is `forge-commit-identity {base}..{head}`. A `forge-` tool
  runs from this installation, anything else from the checkout. A tool
  that exits 3 refuses for now, not for good, as
  `forge-closing-keywords` does while GitHub's closingIssuesReferences
  lags a body edit: the hand-back is said once, and the gate is asked
  again 5 minutes (GATE_RETRY) after it was last asked.

The checkout is the cron checkout (`$FORGE_CRON_CHECKOUT`), kept at
`origin/main` by `forge-cron-run`, and the gate tools run there, so a
pull request can't change the rule it is judged by.

**A head it has waited on for 30 minutes (`merge.stall_minutes`) is
`STALLED`.** When the same
head of the same pull request has had no final `vm/build-and-test`
status, or an unfinished `ci` run, for `STALL_AFTER`, the tick prints a
`STALLED` line and exits 2. It doesn't hand the pull request back,
since the author can't fix a stuck check, and the pull request stays
first in the queue. A write GitHub goes on refusing on the same head
for `STALL_AFTER` is `STALLED` too, with the latest refusal quoted; it
stays first, since the author can't fix the Merger's access either, and
the line says to look at that access only when GitHub refused it so
(401, 403). One refused because the head GitHub expected didn't match
(above), with the nudge not helping or GitHub's head and branch
agreeing, is the exception: nothing the Merger does changes GitHub's
mind, so in the tick it reaches `STALL_AFTER` its `STALLED` line gives
both SHAs, it goes back to its author (once per approval and head; a
push makes GitHub read the head again) and the queue moves past it.
That hand-back is not the pull request's own failure, so it never
counts toward `merge.yield_after_hand_back`. `waiting.json` in the
state directory says since when the tick has waited on which head. A
head first in the batch (`merge.test_ahead.batch`) is never checked on
its own: it waits for the batch top's prediction, whose tree includes
it. Once the build check has started that top (its `ahead_started`),
the wait is timed from the later of the top's start and the wait's, so
a top that is running isn't called a stall, and one that hangs still
is, `STALL_AFTER` after it started. A slot running the top's tree at
another commit (an earlier prediction, which keeps the check from
starting the top) counts as the top's start. A head kept off train 1
while its run goes on (it would spend that run, "waits for its train")
is timed from that run's start the same way. With
`merge.stall_run_factor` (default 1.5), a wait on checks is timed
against that many times the median of the build check's recent runs
when that is longer than `STALL_AFTER`, which stays the floor.

**`next.json` names the pull request the tick stopped at**: `{"at":
..., "pr": N}`, the first in the queue it neither handed back nor
skipped, whether it updates, merges or waits on it, or `"pr": null`
when there is none. Every live tick writes it, and a dry run never
does. It also lists, as `"blocked": [N, ...]`, the approved pull
requests held for a reason only another merge clears (`Blocked-by:` an
open one, or stacked on one), when there are any; the build check
starts their heads after other approved ones. With `checks.merger_first` on, the build check (`forge-pr-check`)
reads it and checks that pull request's head before any other, since
the next merge waits on that verdict.

**With `merge.stall_alert`, a stall is told to people, not only
logged.** A `STALLED` line reaches nobody unless something reads the
log. On its first `STALLED` tick for a pull request and head, the
Merger opens an issue titled `stall_alert.title`, labelled
`stall_alert.labels`, and messages each agent in `stall_alert.tell`
through the XMPP relay, with the router's own sender
(`relay.url`, `relay.token_file`; `$FORGE_ROUTER_RELAY` overrides the
url). It sends the message again every `stall_alert.retell_minutes`
while the stall lasts, or on the next tick if nobody took it. It adds
`labels.waiting_on_owner` once the wait reaches
`stall_alert.owner_after_minutes`. When the stall ends, it comments
how, takes that label off, and closes the issue. `stall-alert.json` in
the state directory holds the open issue, its pull request and head,
and when it was last told. A pull request in the batch `ahead.json`
names is said to wait on that batch's top, by its span and sha
(agent-forge #285). The stall gets its own issue so that no
wait recorded on another item can hide it. Without `stall_alert`
nothing is opened or sent; a dry run never opens, labels, closes or
sends anything either. Only `main` passes the relay sender, so a test
that ticks through a stall messages nobody.

**With `merge.test_ahead`, it predicts the commits the next pull
requests will be tested at** (default off). Under branch protection
that requires a head to be up to date, the queue is serial: update,
wait for the build check, merge, and only then start on the next. This
removes the wait without changing what a merge requires. On a live
tick that is waiting on a pull request (Q0), it writes, for Q0 and up
to `test_ahead.depth` (default 3) behind it, the commit each will have
once those in front of it have merged:

* S0 stands in for Q0's squash commit: the tree `git merge-tree
  --write-tree` gives for Q0's head on `main`'s tip (its own tree when
  it is up to date), with that tip as its parent. A squash of an
  up-to-date head has the head's tree.
* Each later Sk is `git merge-tree --write-tree` of S(k-1) with Qk's
  head, with S(k-1) as its parent: the tree GitHub's `update-branch`
  makes for Qk once the ones ahead have merged.

A pull request that conflicts with those predicted ahead of it is
left out, and the list goes on past it, so one conflict doesn't stop
the rest being tested ahead; a Q0 that conflicts with `main` ends it.
Pull requests already handed back under their current approval and
head are left out; one a `merge.gate` tool refused rejoins once its
title or body changes, since a gate may read them and the author's fix
for, say, an undeclared closing keyword is an edit of the body, and one
a gate refused for now (exit 3) rejoins once the gate, asked again,
passes. The
commits never leave this host: they are local
commits under `refs/agent-forge-merger/ahead/<k>` in this checkout,
with a fixed author, committer and date and no signature, so the same
inputs give the same SHA and a prediction is never tested twice. Their
list is `ahead.json` in the state directory (`at`, `base`, `repo`,
`entries` of `pr`, `head`, `sha`, `tree`, and `same_as_head` when the
tree is the head's own, which the head's own check already tests, or
`restacked` when it is a stack's layer predicted as the Merger will
restack it: merge.stacks.predict_restacked, designs/0042).
Every live tick rewrites both and empties them when it waits on
nothing; a dry run writes neither. `forge-pr-check` tests the entries
in its free slots and posts green on a head whose tree one of them, or
any other check here, already turned green.

**Why that is safe.** A merge tree is a function of its inputs, so a
prediction that matches is exactly the code the update produces. The
prediction matters only when it is right: if GitHub's merge ever
produces a different tree, that tree is simply not one the build check
has tested, and the pull request is checked as it would be without
`test_ahead`. Nothing is written to GitHub that isn't written without
it, the gate is unchanged, and green is only ever posted for a tree a
real run turned green. What it costs is build time: a hand-back or a
push spends the predictions behind it, at most `depth` + 1 runs.

**With `merge.test_ahead.batch` above 1, one run covers several
merges** (designs/0032; default 1, off). The first `batch` predictions
are the batch, and the last of them its top: `ahead.json` says so as
`batch` (`size`, and the batch's `trees`). `forge-pr-check` runs only
the top, and once the top is green it posts green on each member's
head as it is updated, `green: covered by batch ...`. The batch goes
on, tick to tick, while what is left of it still leads the predictions
unchanged; a push, a hand-back or a new order starts a new one. When
the build check's state has the top red, the batch is halved, rounding
up, until its top isn't red or it is one pull request, which is then
checked as without batches: `BATCH #a..#b: top red, checking #a..#m`.
A green top says `BATCH #a..#b: top <sha> green, merging N`. Each line
is said once. A head whose green says it was covered is merged only
while the last tick's batch still holds: that batch has its tree, its
top's tree is green in the build check's state, and every pull request
after it in the batch is still in the queue at the head predicted.
Otherwise it waits for the new batch's top, or a run of its own tree
(a batch split down to it), so the default branch's
tip is never a tree no run tested unless a member is handed back
after others merged. The build check's state is read from its state
directory, as for `merge.queued_stall_minutes`.

**With `merge.test_ahead.batches` above 1, batches run side by side**
(designs/0035 part 2; default 1). Up to that many batches are predicted
end to end, each from the top of the one before, and those after the
first are `later` in `ahead.json` (`size`, `trees`) and the record.
`forge-pr-check` runs their tops in spare slots. Once a later batch's
top has started (the build check's `ahead_started`, or a verdict on its
tree), it is `fixed`: its size stays while its trees still come next.
The first that isn't fixed takes newly ready pull requests at its end,
up to `batch`. When the batch before has merged, a fixed one is the
batch at its own size, so its top's green covers it at once. Nothing
follows a red first batch, or a later one whose top is red.

**A batch whose top's run has started keeps its order**
(`merge.test_ahead.fixed_order`, default on; designs/0044, rule 3).
Every batch up to the last whose top's run has started (`fixed_order`)
keeps its members' order: an overdue candidate, right of way's holder,
one newly approved and a returning stack layer go behind them, and
right of way holds none of them. Only urgent ones, behind the batch
being merged, and a red default branch's fixes go in front, as before.
A member that drops out ends it from its batch on. The record lists
them under `fixed`. With `unstable_after_recuts: N` (off by default;
rule 5), a tick that re-cuts such a batch says `"recut": true`, and
while more than N ticks in the last hour did, only
`batches_when_unstable` (default 1) batches are predicted (`unstable`
in the record; `UNSTABLE` and `SETTLED` lines).

**A member is checked before its batch** (designs/0035, fourth
amendment). Before predicting, the Merger runs, for each pull request
it would predict on the default branch, the checks that don't depend
on its place: the approval covers the head, and each `merge.gate`
tool. One that fails is handed back at once, as at merge time, with
`CHECKED-AHEAD #n: <reason>; handed back before its batch`, and isn't
predicted. Results are kept in `checked.json` by head, approved commit
and description, so each runs once; merge time checks again.

**A red batch keeps its suspects** (designs/0035, second amendment).
When a batch's top is red, its members become its suspects, kept in
`suspects.json` with their heads and the red top's tree, and the batch
isn't halved. While there are suspects they lead the queue in their
order, and nothing is predicted in front of them or among them: a
layer or a pull request newly ready goes behind them. A suspect that
is pushed to, handed back, unapproved or closed leaves. Each tick the
suspects' prefixes, nested predictions, are judged by the build
check's verdicts on their trees: the longest green one is the batch
and merges, and the shortest red one bounds the search. The prefixes
between them are tested at evenly spaced cut points, one in the
batch's slot and the rest as `later` batches, `batches` - 1 of them
(at least one), so `forge-pr-check` runs them side by side; the whole
set, already red, isn't run again. A prediction for a prefix longer
than a red one is listed as `stop` in `ahead.json`, and the build
check stops its run. A red prefix one longer than a green one names
its last member, and once it leads, `SUSPECTS #a..#b: #n is red on its
own; the search ends`. With `merge.test_ahead.hand_back_red_suspect`
(default on; designs/0044, rule 4) it is handed back as soon as it is
named, `SUSPECT-RED #n: ...`, and kept in `red-suspects.json`, with
when it was found red and the tip it was found red on: until it has a
new head it is neither predicted, updated nor merged. A green run of
the same head also releases it (amendment, 2026-11-04), as
`labels.rerun` gives one: the build check's verdict on the head, from
its state, finished after the suspect was found red, merged with that
tip or a later one. The tick logs `SUSPECT-CLEARED #n: green rerun at
<head>`, says so once on the pull request, and the hand-back is
forgotten, so it is predicted again under its approval. A suspect
kept before the amendment, with no time, waits for a new head.
Without it, it is checked and handed back as without batches. When no
suspects are left and every one merged, the first red was flaky:
`BATCH-FLAKE #a..#b: every suspect merged green; the red top's tree
<tree> was a flake`.

**A red that is only the base's names no suspect** (designs/0011,
amendment). When every step a batch top's tree failed also failed in
the default branch's own run of the base it was predicted on, the top
is taken as not yet judged: no search starts, nothing is handed back,
and `BATCH-BASE-RED #a..#b: red only in <steps>, as <base> is` is
logged once. A head whose own check is red only where the tip it was
merged with is red waits instead of being handed back, while the
red-default-branch issue is open, and is updated (checked afresh) once
it is closed. **Urgent pull requests are batched apart**: a batch, the
first or a later one, ends where its members change between urgent and
not, so a pin change at the front doesn't wait on, or get searched
with, the rest.

**With `merge.test_ahead.stacks`, a stack's ready layers join their
base's batch** (designs/0035): each is predicted straight after the
pull request it is stacked on, while it would be eligible but for its
base and its approval covers its head. A layer joins only a batch not
yet being run. The batch holds while the layer is still ready at the
head predicted, held as stacked until the layer below merges. A queued
pull request a layer pushed out of the batch is `owed` (ahead.json),
and no layer pushes an owed one out again. Layers side by side on one
base (amendment of 2026-11-12) are taken in number order, depth first:
a layer, the layers on it, then the next one beside it; one that isn't
ready, or in a train won't merge onto what is ahead of it, holds back
the layers on it and no others, and a train member that leaves takes
only the layers on it with it (ahead.json's entries say `on`, the
member each is stacked on). With `layers_need_own_green` false (default
true; with `merge.own_head_green`), a layer boards without its own
build check's green, its train's top testing it; once its base has
merged it stays a candidate without one while its head is the one it
boarded with, or that one brought up to date (`own_green_wait`'s
`boarded`), and `train_merge` takes the layers held as stacked as
members, so the train goes in as one. With `own_green_to_board` false
(default true; with `merge.own_head_green` and `trains: "live"`), so
does any pull request on the default branch held only for its own
green: it is tried for a train after the candidates, and once aboard
it is a candidate while its head is the one it boarded with, or that
one brought up to date (`boarded_members`). One whose own head is red
still doesn't board, and a red train is searched as any (amendment of
2026-11-12, ninth).

**With `merge.test_ahead.trains`, batches are trains** (designs/0047;
default off). `"live"`: each batch is a train of carriages, a stack's
ready layers with their base (`stacks`) or one pull request, cut once in
the queue's order, urgent ones first: the first carriage that merges
cleanly onto the train's base (the default branch's tip, then each
train's top), then each next one that touches no file the train touches
(`trains_within: "clean"`: merges cleanly), up to `batch` pull requests,
urgent ones only with urgent ones. A stack is never split; one longer
than `batch` is a train of its own. A layer that is red, or won't merge
onto the layers below it, stays behind with those above it
(`TRAIN-LEFT`), and the layers below it board without them: a layer
holds nothing under it back. One that conflicts with a train ahead waits
for a later train: it is not handed back for that. A cut train is fixed:
ahead.json says `"trains": true`, its entries their `train` and
`carriage`, and every train counts as started (`fixed_order`), so
nothing joins it or moves into it. A member that leaves, by a push to a
tree other than the one predicted, a hand-back or a lost approval, goes
alone, with the layers of its stack above it: `TRAIN-LEFT #n: pushed
<sha> mid-train`. The Merger's own RESTACK of a layer is not a push:
with STACK-UPDATE still to come, it is in flight. Nor is any head that
is the boarded one brought up to date and nothing else (`own_push`:
clean merges of what the default branch already has, whichever stack
the tick acts on), nor one whose prediction is unchanged: predicted
where the boarded head was, it gives the same tree (`same_prediction`:
an author's merge of the default branch made any other way, with the
default branch as the first parent, say, or of the head of a member
ahead of it in the train), said as `TRAIN-KEPT #n: pushed <sha>, same
tree in its place`; its prediction is the same commit, so
the top's run still counts, and a train merged as one (`train_merge`)
merges its new head. A restacked layer stays a candidate meanwhile: its
build check's and gated instances' verdicts on the head before the
restack still count (`on_main`). The rest keep their
places, and the train's top, now a new tree, is run again. When one
leaves the first train after its top's run has started (and isn't red),
the members below it are kept with that top (ahead.json's `left_top`;
`merge.test_ahead.left_top_covers`, agent-forge #296): the old top's
tree held each of theirs as the new train does, and a prediction's run
is never stopped, so once it is green they merge as one train
(`TRAIN-UNDER-LEFT #a..#b`) without waiting for the new top's run. A
pull request that is urgent or fixes a red default branch and is in no train
cuts every train after the first again, and the default branch moving to
a tree no train predicted cuts them all (`TRAIN-RECUT: ...`). A red
top's members are the suspects, as a red batch's, a stack's layers held
as stacked among them, each where its train had it (`with_suspects`).
The trains are kept through the search (ahead.json's `kept_trains`) and
taken up again when it ends: the one found red leaves, those merged are
gone, the rest keep their places, and new pull requests go into later
trains (`trains_before`; amendment of 2026-11-12, seventh). A suspect
brought up to date and nothing else stays a suspect at its new head
(`suspects_left`). A later train's green top covers that
train and every one in front of it (amendment of 2026-11-11): its tree
contains them all, and its run took in every step their changes touch,
so they are folded into the first batch and start merging at once,
without waiting for the tops in front of it, whose runs are then spent;
a red top in front of it is overruled, as a flake. A pull request whose
own head is red doesn't board while the default branch is green,
unless it was red only for a conflict with an older tip (amendment of
2026-11-12, eighth); the record's `train_waits` says why each that
didn't board waits. One that conflicts with the
default branch only where `merge.resolve` or its `union` would resolve
it can't board as it stands, and train members stay ahead of it, so a
live tick resolves the first such pull request ahead of its turn, and it
boards at the next cut. New trains are said as `TRAIN-CUT k: #a #b`; the
record has `trains` and `train_events`. `"dry-run"` changes nothing: the
batches are cut as without it, and the trains this tick would have, from
the dry run's own (`trains-dry.json`), are said as `TRAIN-DRY 1: #a #b |
2: #c` when they change, each change as `TRAIN-DRY LEFT ...`, and kept
in the record as `trains` with `"trains_dry": true`, with
`recut_avoided` when the batches were cut again but no train was.
With `train_merge` (amendment of 2026-11-11, default off), a green
train goes in as one: while the default branch is still the tip it was
predicted from, a chain of merge commits, one per member with its head
as a parent and the tree its prediction tested, is pushed to
`train_branch` + `<first>-<last>`, given `checks.context`'s green, and
merged by one pull request as a merge commit pinned to its top
(`TRAIN-MERGE`). GitHub then marks each member merged; any it doesn't,
and any train it refuses, merge one at a time as before. A member of
the first train behind the default branch is not updated meanwhile
(amendment of 2026-11-12): the update would change nothing the train
merges and only start a build of its new head, so it waits for the
top (`WAIT`, "in a train of N that merges as one"). A train of one, a
red top, and a train GitHub refused this tick are updated as before.
With `tell_boarded` (agent-forge #286), each pull request that boards a
live train is told once, in a `Not-a-review` comment, not to push to it
unless it is handed back (`TOLD-BOARDED #<pr> <sha>`, the record's
`told_boarded`); the Merger's own updates don't repeat it, and one that
leaves the trains is told again when it reboards.

**With `merge.hold_conflicting`, a pull request that conflicts with
one ahead of it waits for that one instead of going round again**
(default off). An author with several approved pull requests that
change the same lines (a generated file, say) otherwise has every one
handed back after each merge: each is rebased, approved again and
rebuilt, and the next merge does it all again. With it, each tick
checks every pull request in the queue but the first against the ones
ahead of it that are not themselves held this way, with `git merge-tree
--write-tree` of the two heads. One that conflicts with one ahead (Q)
is held, `held` saying `conflicts with #Q, ahead of it in the queue`
(never behind a Q that conflicts with `main`, which goes back to its
author):
it is neither updated, merged, handed back nor predicted. A live tick
says so on it once per head and Q, signed like a hand-back, asking the
author to leave it until Q merges; `held-conflicting.json` in the
state directory remembers what was said, and the record's `holds` what
each tick held. Once Q has merged it is judged as usual, and goes back
to its author if it then conflicts with `main`: one rebase instead of
one per merge. Two heads git can't compare are not held.

**With `merge.code_owners`, a code owner's word is needed** (default
off). A candidate that changes (since `main`) a path the default
branch's CODEOWNERS (`.github/`, the root or `docs/`, the first found)
gives owners is held, `held` saying `waits for code owner @owner:
path`, until for each such path one of its owners wrote it or has
approved: their newest review is `APPROVED`, at a commit that covers
the head as a reviewer's must. Owners are `@login`s; a team's or an
email's path waits for good, since the Merger can't check it. A path
whose changes can't be listed waits too. It is a hold, not a
hand-back: nothing is said, and the author isn't asked to do anything.
With `merge.code_owner_ask`, the hold opens an issue for the owner,
once per pull request and head, closed when the hold ends.

**With `merge.yield_ignores_conflicts`, a conflict with `main` doesn't
count toward the yield** (default false). A conflict is mostly what
merged after the approval, not the pull request's own failure.

**With `merge.right_of_way`, a pull request overtaken again and again
gets to land** (default off; designs/0002). One handed back to its
author for a conflict with `main` at `after_conflicts` heads (default
2) in the last 24 hours has earned it, and has it for its head H while
H is clean against `main`, H wasn't handed back for anything else, and
less than `minutes` (default 60) has passed since a tick first saw H
with it earned (`right-of-way.json` in the state directory remembers
when). Meanwhile every other candidate whose head conflicts with H is
held, `held` saying `conflicts with #P, which has right of way`, told
once like a hold above; once P is a candidate it goes first, after
only the one a `test_ahead` run is in flight for, and never yields.
With `while_eligible` (default on; designs/0044, rule 2), P holds
nobody while it isn't a candidate at H or was handed back at H, and
the record's `right_of_way` says why under `idle`.
The record's `right_of_way` says who has it, `{pr, head, since}`, or
null. Of several that earned it, the first in the order above has it.

**With `merge.age_first`, a pull request that has waited long goes
first** (default off; designs/0037). A tick remembers when it first
saw each pull request as a candidate (`waiting-since.json` in the state
directory), through hand-backs and new heads, until it merges, closes,
or has been no candidate for 24 hours. One that has waited
`after_minutes` is overdue (the record's `overdue`, minutes waited):
it goes ahead of every candidate but the one in flight, urgent ones
and the members of a batch being merged, the longest waiting first
(an overdue member keeps its place in the batch, whose green covers
its trees in that order only);
predictions are never made past it while it conflicts with one ahead;
a newer one's right of way doesn't hold it; and it never yields.

**With `test_ahead`, the pull request a run is in flight for keeps its
place.** When the last tick updated or waited on a pull request that
is still a candidate, it stays first, and no blocker (a pull request
another one's `Blocked-by:` names) is promoted past it. A blocker that
jumped it would merge, move `main` and put it behind again, spending
the run it was already having. One merged or handed back keeps
nothing. `next.json` then also says that tick's `action`.

**With `test_ahead.green_batch_first`, a green batch goes before it**
(designs/0047, amendment of 2026-11-10). When the last tick predicted
the batch on the default branch's tip as it is now, the batch's top is
green, and the one in flight is not a member and has no verdict on its
head yet, the one in flight keeps no place and the batch merges
first, logged `BATCH-FIRST`. Waiting for its run would hold a batch
that is ready, and its merge would move `main` under the green top
and spend it; updated onto the tip, it loses only the run it was
having.

**With `test_ahead`, those already predicted keep their order.** The
candidates the last live tick predicted (`ahead.json`), at the heads
it predicted, go first, in its order, after only the one in flight.
Their runs have started: anything else goes behind them, whether
approved later, an older one approved again, one newly labelled
urgent, a blocker or one with right of way, rather than into the
middle, where it would spend every prediction behind it. One with a
new head loses its place, and one left out as a conflict goes behind.

Exit 0 when the tick ran, whatever GitHub answered its write, or
another tick held the lock; 2 when it could not ask (no checkout, no
token, GitHub or git failed) or the head it waits on is `STALLED`.
"""

